  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1215 AM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nWarm & Windy Today:\n\nA phasing, negatively tilted mid/upper level trough on GOES \nwater vapor imagery loops is seen progressing through the Rocky \nMountain West. Accompanying surface cyclonic flow is expected to\nlift northeast through the Dakotas today, limiting local \nimpacts to increasing sky cover, further warming of anomalous \ntemperatures as the associated low level warm sector shifts east\nthrough tonight, and strong south-southwest winds through much \nof the day, locally strongest west of the Mississippi River \nValley in southeast Minnesota with gusts near 35+mph possible. \nDaytime highs from the mid 50s to low 60s expected today through\nSaturday.\n\nWeekend Precipitation Potential:\n\nWarmest temperatures expected Friday from additional low level \nWAA, ushering in 5-10C 850mb temperatures, 10-15C above normal \nfor this time of year. Subsequent precipitation chances again \nappear later compared to previous forecasts as LREF member \ndProg/dt trends push higher probabilities (80%) for 0.1\" in 24 \nhours not until Saturday night. A blend of previous EPS/GEFS \nruns appears the most likely solution, deeper/quicker trends \nwithin each respective model runs albeit farther northwest of \nthe local forecast area. Therefore, timing and extent of local \nprecipitation chances are dependent on an additional longwave \ntrough sagging southeast through southern Canada, providing the \nzonal push for the baroclinic boundary through the Upper \nMississippi River Valley. Confidence for 0.1\" to 0.25\" across \nmuch of the area, similar to previous forecasts.\n\nMore Seasonable Temperatures Into Early Next Week:\n\nCooler temperatures expected through the end of the weekend \ninto early next week from the passing frontal boundary. Current \nNBM forecast for daytime highs are in the 30s and overnight lows\nin the 20s.",
